report=f'''# Valoria V2 final release acceptance — 2026-10-12

**PASS — the Portal live-acquisition blocker is resolved, the controlled API release is deployed, and deployed compatibility checks pass.** There are no remaining blockers for this API release. The V2 frontend passed its build and controlled browser checks; public frontend publication remains outside this API deployment. Product scope and investigation limits below remain open and are not represented as completed features.

## Precise failure and correction

The candidate opened a fresh anonymous httpx client. The working scheduled Portal scraper instead uses the host session runner and a verified cookie loader. The candidate neither loaded that session nor had its private directory mounted. The anonymous detail request received upstream403; the old parser returned None and lookup classified it as failed, which the API converted to generic502. The observed502 therefore masked an upstream denial caused by divergent acquisition architecture. It was not evidence of an expired production session or a local parser exception. The inspected session contained a valid, unexpired ssid; no reauthentication circuit was set. Session values were never printed or exported.

The API now delegates session validation to the same existing loader, uses the production request headers, and reads the existing private session through a read-only mount. The scheduler remains the session writer. No browser infrastructure, reauthentication, challenge solving, retries, proxy rotation, paid dependency, worker change or resource allocation increase was introduced. Missing/invalid session and the reauthentication circuit fail closed before acquisition. At most four source-validated redirects are followed under the existing SSRF protections.

Error mappings are explicit: unavailable404/410→404, access denial/session/reauth/rate limit→503, upstream timeout→504, transport/upstream server failure→502. Structured provider messages reach the frontend instead of unrelated UF/auth text. Error responses never fabricate an active listing. Nullable room/parking counts follow the existing form contract while raw missing-count warnings preserve uncertainty.

